00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:02.909 align:start position:0%
 
hey everyone this is kevin from  thechesswebsite.com so many people have  been interested in the petrov defense  lately it got me thinking  i should definitely make a video on the  immortal game  of alexander petrov who is the  individual that the petrov defense is  named after now petrov if you're not  familiar with him lived in the 1700  1800 time frame russian chess master for  50 years  he was the number one chess player in  russia which is a long time knowing that  there's so many good players  coming out of russia in 1844 he's  playing an individual  play by the name of alexander hoffman  another chess master both these players  in warsaw at the time that they played  this match but white's going to be  played by alexander hoffman and black's  going to be played by  alexander petrov hoffman starts out with  e4 and then e5 from petrov knight to f3  and you can already see has the  potential to play knight f6 but instead  decides to play  knight to c6 bishop to c4 the italian  game  bishop to c5 getting into the gioco  piano and then c3 this is the main line  preparing for  d4 trying to control the center of the  board for whites  also has different options here you can  always play the evans game but  sacrificing that pawn and material  maybe the bishop comes back here to a5  and then you push forward with d4 this  is the  common theme that white's looking to do  is play c3 and then  push forward with d4 and that's the same  thing in the main line of c3  next move of d4 here and we see petrov  play  knight to f6 and then d4 pawn takes here  on d4 and then hoffman has  couple options you have the main line  which same as what we talked about pawn  takes here on d4  white has two central pawns compared to  black only has  one central pawn here so that's kind of  the main idea but hoffman goes for  also an extremely popular play and that  is  e5 now there's not as much theory in  this opening in 1844 so the fact that he  was able to find this move that  so many players play today is really  interesting to me when you just study  these old games and you find these lines  that these players were actually able to  find  back in the day pretty cool stuff to see  so pawn to  e5 and then from here  really you'll see most players playing  pawn to  d5 from black this is attacking the  bishop right here  says hey if you want to take this knight  that's fine we'll go ahead and take that  bishop  but instead we see petrov playing knight  to e4 then a pretty interesting move in  the game and that is bishop to  d5 because this really is almost  baiting this knight here on e4 to take  on f2 if we start to look at how black  can really  move this knight here on e4 okay well it  can't just come back here to d6 or  f6 that's being protected by this pawn  here on e5  it can't come here to g5 because white  has both the bishop here and  the knight both attacking it so that's  not gonna work  okay we can clearly see that can't come  to c3  or d2 it's just going to fall so the  only real option is to play knight takes  here on f2 you can always try something  crazy  pawn to f5 trying to protect it but then  just the pawn takes here  controlling the center of the board two  central pawns  maybe bishop to b4 but then after a  bishop to d2  just what does black really have and  then  has a pretty exposed king side over here  without a really strong attack so i  don't think that's  a good option for black so in the game  petrov decides hey if you want to give  me that  knight takes here on f2 we'll go ahead  and down go down that path so knight  takes on f2  and then king takes here on f2  now black takes on c3 and part of the  reason  you may say why gobble up material uh  this is a discovered attack with the  bishop here on c5 the entire reason you  would give up your knight here on f2  is one to expose the king and then two  to really have a strong attack so  if you're gonna do this you have to be  pretty aggressive which petrov  uh definitely intends to do for his  immortal game so the king comes to g3  could also just come back to  the first rank here both are fine from  white's perspective  and then pawn takes here on b2 attacking  the rook and the bishop essentially  forcing white to do something here  and part of the the reason that you want  to do this  is to get this dark square bishop off of  this diagonal right here so uh  definitely  something that white has to keep in mind  as far as where his material is  and then how he's going to defend some  of the attacks that black has  on board so next move is knight to e7  this is both attacking this bishop here  on  d5 but more importantly it's coming up  here to  f5 attacking the king here on  g3 now white plays the move knight to  g5 and the thought really is here okay  if you want to  take with the knight here on d5  well now then the knight's gonna come  down to f7  this is attacking the queen really not  just going to lose your queen so you're  probably going to take with your king  here on  f7 and then queen takes here on d5  check and then after the king moves the  queen takes here on c5  both players are playing extremely high  level and they just say okay that was a  pretty fun exchange here  let's see how the end of the game goes  and then they just kind of give each  other a nod and things continue but  petrov had other plans after knight  takes here on f7  he has no intention of taking this  knight here on f7  he intends to give up this queen here on  d8 so he decides to go ahead and castle  on the king side  so yeah go ahead and take my queen here  on d8 which hoffman does he takes knight  takes on  d8 and then bishop to f2 now this is  check and you can start to see  the threat here this king can't come to  the ephile because of the rook  can't take the bishop because of the  rook it can't come to  h4 and so really the only options are  g4 and h3  but either one can so he decides to go  to h3 but then  pawn to d6 and now the light square  bishop and gets involved into the game  with a check here so now pawn to  e6 knight to f4  and you can see this is one of the  reasons why you want to play that pawn  to  b2 attacking the rook on  a1 here and it got that bishop off of  the dark square diagonal so really  thinking ahead as far as how he wanted  to attack says yeah  ha if you want to just give me that rook  that's fine  if you just want to give me a free pawn  that's fine as well but you probably  need to take and that's going to set up  a threat  later down in the game so after knight 2  f4  check king 2 g4 and then knight  takes here on e6 and while this is not  check it's  it's gearing up for a strong attack  coming on the next move from black white  decides to go ahead and take here on e6  there's no real good move  for white black really has all the  momentum here and there's no way to stop  it but decides to go ahead and  play here on e6 and then bishop takes  here on e6  king to g5 rook up here to f5  it is all forced from here king to g4  pawn to h5 king to h3  and then rook to f3 checkmate pretty  cool  checkmate you usually don't see the  bishop coming down here on f2  and then having the king just be chased  around  on the king side of the board in the  middle of the board  with bishops and a rook so  really exciting game from alexander  petrov did the whole thing  in 20 moves so it's nice to have an  immortal game  and get through such a fun variation  with  only 20 moves so really cool hopefully  you guys  enjoyed this video i always like looking  at some of these older immortal games  and seeing how people approach the game  and seeing the variations that they went  down uh so thanks for watching i'll see  you guys  in the next video

